From ffb355b59a59c01b53ec2cffb133a6c6e6b6e6f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kirk <hello@kirkdiamond.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 10:50:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] drm/amd/display: latch both iMac 5K tiles on the same
 frame on DCE

amdgpu_dm_commit_planes() programs one CRTC at a time, and each
dc_update_planes_and_stream() takes and drops the update lock of its
own pipe. On the merged 5K output the left tile is programmed first and
unlocked, and a VSync before the right tile is programmed latches the
left half of a frame one refresh before the right half: a one-frame
tear at the seam, and the commit completes a frame late.

DCE has no interdependent_update_lock() that would lock both pipes.
Add dc_imac5k_hold_tile_locks() and dc_imac5k_release_tile_locks(),
which take the update lock of both tiles' pipes and keep it held: while
held, dce_pipe_control_lock() leaves those pipes alone. DM takes the
locks before programming the tiles of a fast update and releases them
once both are programmed, so both are latched on the same VSync. The
right tile also skips the wait for the target vblank, which the left
tile has already done and which would otherwise sleep with the locks
held.

The locks are only held for fast updates without a modeset, during
which DC does not wait for a flip to latch, and only on DCE; DCN takes
its locks through interdependent_update_lock().

Written with an AI coding assistant (Claude Code, Claude Opus 5.5)
working from a hand-written plan; tested on an iMac Pro (iMacPro1,1,
Fedora 45): both tiles latch on the same frame, no tear at
the seam.

[Fedora 7.3 backport: 7.3 has no hwss_pipe_control_lock(), DCE pipes
 are locked through dce_pipe_control_lock(), which skips held pipes.]

Signed-off-by: Kirk <hello@kirkdiamond.com>
